# Line endings and per-path behaviour for THIS REPOSITORY -- a committed contract,
# not core.autocrlf, which is per-machine and cannot be reviewed or enforced.
# Verify with:  git ls-files --eol      (i/ = index blob, w/ = working tree, attr/ = this file)
# After editing: git add --renormalize . && git status && commit that ALONE.
#
# ONE LINE PER EXTENSION. a brace group like  *.[cmd,bat]  with curly brackets matches NOTHING here -- wildmatch has no brace
# expansion (it IS required in .editorconfig; keep the two files in step).
#
# Decided 2026-09-10 from `git ls-files --eol` over 6955 files: 1004 Lua blobs LF and
# 228 CRLF in the index while every one is CRLF on disk only because of autocrlf; two
# Python files CRLF on disk for the same reason; one .dbt with mixed endings and 39 that
# are UTF-16. See docs/AI_ARCHITECTURE.md §5 and the README "Database tables" section.

# ---- baseline: LF in the index for anything git detects as text
*                    text=auto

# ---- read by the game engine from disk, shipped CRLF by the base game: CRLF in the
#      working tree on EVERY platform, so a Linux CI checkout packages what Windows runs
*.lua                text eol=crlf
*.txt                text eol=crlf
*.ini                text eol=crlf
*.cmd                text eol=crlf
*.bat                text eol=crlf

# ---- database tables: the exact bytes ARE the content. DB/Languages/**/Text.dbt and
#      Names_*.dbt are UTF-16LE with a BOM; #french/Tables.dbt has mixed endings as
#      upstream shipped it; 27 core tables are LF and the engine runs them as they are.
#      Never `text` these -- an explicit text attribute would convert the UTF-16 files
#      and corrupt them. `diff` keeps the ASCII ones reviewable as text.
*.dbt                -text diff

# ---- tooling and docs, run or read on the dev box: LF everywhere, Windows included
*.py                 text eol=lf diff=python
*.sh                 text eol=lf diff=bash
*.md                 text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.json               text eol=lf
LICENSE              text eol=lf
.gitattributes       text eol=lf
.gitignore           text eol=lf
.editorconfig        text eol=lf

# ---- the engine's own API dump: 1 MB of HTML with a NUL in it, so git already treats
#      it as binary (trap 5). Generated, never hand-edited; say so rather than let a
#      future cleanup "fix" it.
ScriptDocumentation.html   -text

# ---- game asset formats: binary (= -text -diff -merge). Git sniffs the NUL byte and
#      gets every one of these right already; the lines are documentation, and they
#      protect a future variant that happens to be printable ASCII. `*.git` is the
#      world-editor map format under Worlds/Editor-Files, not anything of git's.
*.ogg                binary
*.mp3                binary
*.wav                binary
*.tga                binary
*.dds                binary
*.bmp                binary
*.png                binary
*.jpg                binary
*.nif                binary
*.kf                 binary
*.kfm                binary
*.cmf                binary
*.cm2                binary
*.gui                binary
*.gso                binary
*.sco                binary
*.wld                binary
*.bse                binary
*.dat                binary
*.git                binary
*.exe                binary
*.dll                binary

# ---- keep development scaffolding out of `git archive`. Here, and ONLY here, a
#      directory is `dir/` -- `dir/**` would leave an empty directory in the tarball.
/.gitattributes      export-ignore
/.editorconfig       export-ignore
/tools/              export-ignore
/meta/               export-ignore
/docs/               export-ignore

# ---- 3ds Max ASCII scene export shipped with a world: bytes are the content
*.ASE                -text
*.ase                -text

# ---- generated tables and the one markdown file with the other extension
*.tsv                text eol=lf
*.mkd                text eol=lf diff=markdown
